g  to each other and through their  professional societies and one of the  points kindly inquisitors makes is  you've got to allow the system enough  space to turn aside sometimes quite  brutally people who the system decides  don't have good  and and oftentimes the system will be in  error because we you know I mean you  mentioned Hayek before he's a guy in the  40s and 50s it was kind of on the edges  of the economics and social science  world and you know by the early 70s that  won a Nobel Prize so the great advantage  of of liberal science of our knowledge  making system is not that it does not  make mistakes it's that it catches  mistakes very very quickly talk a bit  about Frank Kameny what does his life an  example tell us about the best way  forward in a world where knowledge is  likely to be more and more recognized as  provisional and hotly contested and  where freedom is always you know a  couple of people at a back room  somewhere away from being extinguished  so 1957 man named Frank Kameny is fired  from his government job as an astronomer  and instead of rolling over he fights  back he goes to Congress he goes to the  Supreme Court he begins mounting public  protests 1971 he's the first openly gay  candidate for Congress now it seems at  first like he never gets anywhere but in  fact at every stage he was making  powerful moral arguments and gathering  more people behind them and the  wonderful thing about a regime of open  criticism and debate is once it gets  going it is very good at finding that  one guy with the important idea the  Galileo the Copernicus the king the  Kemeny bringing that person to the  center testing those ideas and  discovering if they're true that they  really are better ideas Frank Kameny  pioneering activists live to see his  ideas spectacularly vindicated and to  receive a public apology for his firing  from the US government and he never  called for the restriction or  suppression of speech with which he  disagreed  I knew F